# About This Project
#
# Litex source:
# Canonical module: scripts/Analysis/textbook
#
# This project is an ongoing Litex formalization inspired by Terence Tao's
# Analysis I. It follows the mathematical development of the book in source
# order while expressing its definitions, constructions, and theorems through
# Litex's fact-oriented proof interface. It is an independent translation
# project, not an official edition of or substitute for the original book.
#
# Translation conventions
#
# Standalone items explicitly labelled Exercise or Exercises are outside this
# translation and are omitted rather than replaced by declarations or coverage
# placeholders. A named definition, theorem, proposition, lemma, corollary, or
# remark is still retained when the source merely leaves its proof, or one
# step of its proof, as an exercise; that omission is then shown locally as an
# explicit trust boundary.
#
# This is a source-ordered mathematical translation, not a line-by-line
# transcription. Litex builtins are used for familiar carriers and operations
# such as natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, sets, functions, and
# arithmetic. The book's constructions of those objects may therefore appear
# as an alternate proof route or marked proof debt rather than as prerequisites
# for every later theorem. Routine implicit steps may be expanded, while long
# supporting arguments may be deferred when formalizing them would obscure the
# chapter's main mathematical line.
#
# Inclusion and proof status are separate. An included statement may be fully
# checked, may depend on an explicit axiom or abstract background interface, or
# may contain the narrowest practical `trust` at an unfinished step. `trust`
# records accepted proof debt; it does not mean that Litex verified that step.
# Missing non-exercise source material and remaining proof work are tracked by
# the project ledgers, so the presence of a chapter file does not by itself
# claim complete source coverage.
#
# Most declarations included here are accepted by the Litex verifier. The
# remaining unchecked steps are kept visible as explicit trust boundaries using keyword `trust`.
# Some correspond to arguments that a textbook naturally leaves to the reader;
# others expose missing library support, automation, or proof work in the
# current project. We try to fill in implicit reasoning when it clarifies the
# formal dependencies, but occasionally retain a marked omission to respect
# the book's mathematical pace and keep the formal text readable.
#
# Later chapters offer especially useful case studies: the construction of the
# natural, integer, rational, and real number systems; sequences and series;
# cardinality; continuity; and differentiation. Together they show how Litex
# can grow a substantial mathematical context from readable definitions and
# reusable facts while keeping its assumptions and proof debt inspectable.
#
# We hope readers gain both a clearer view of analysis and a practical sense of
# how ordinary textbook mathematics can become checked formal mathematics,
# including an honest understanding of what Litex can and cannot yet verify.
